# Final Findings

## Overview
This project simulates the investigation and response process for a ransomware-style incident using:
- A synthetic PCAP modeling ransomware command-and-control (C2) beaconing  
- A benign Windows NTFS disk image  
- Host activity artifacts (documents, registry, recent activity)  
- A modeled incident timeline  

This allowed us to practice incident response methodology without handling live malware.

---

## 1. Network Forensics Findings
The synthetic PCAP (`synthetic_ransom_beaconing.pcap`) revealed three suspicious outbound UDP packets from the internal host `192.168.1.10`:

| Time | Source        | Destination      | Port | Payload              |
|------|---------------|------------------|------|----------------------|
| 1s   | 192.168.1.10  | 185.199.110.1    | 80   | `RANSOM_NOTE_CHECK`  |
| 2s   | 192.168.1.10  | 46.4.233.1       | 443  | `RANSOM_STATUS`      |
| 3s   | 192.168.1.10  | 203.0.113.50     | 443  | `RANSOM_PING`        |

### Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)
- External IPs contacted on web ports using **UDP, not TCP**
- High-numbered source ports (4444, 5555)
- Payloads referencing ransomware behavior

This traffic resembles periodic C2 beaconing used by ransomware to:
- Check for ransom note delivery  
- Report encryption progress  
- Ping the attacker’s server  

